<section xml:id="exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise>
        <p>Let <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{3} \to \mathbb{R}^{2}</m> be a linear transformation such that <m>T(1,0,0) = (1,0)</m>, <m>T(0,1,0) = (0,1)</m>, and <m>T(0,0,1) = (1,1)</m>. Write <m>T</m> in matrix form as <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Write the linear transformation <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{3} \to \mathbb{R}^{3}</m> defined by <m>T(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}) = (x_{2},x_{3}, x_{1})</m> as a matrix.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Explain why <m>T: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{n-1}</m> defined by <m>T(x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n}) = (x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n-1})</m> is a linear transformation. What is the matrix representation?</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Explain why <m>f(x,y) = xy</m> is not a linear transformation.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Is <m>f(x,y) = (-y,x)</m> a linear transformation? If so, describe the matrix. If not, explain why not.</p>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <p>Is <m>f(x,y) = \max(x,y)</m> a linear transformation? If so, describe the matrix. If not, explain why not.</p>
      </exercise>
    </section>